\section{Pruning Protocol Details}
\label{app:pruning_details}

Both pruning methods physically remove unselected tokens from the sequence, reducing the actual input length to the LLM rather than zeroing them out; zero-masking preserves positional structure while physical removal does not. After removal, remaining tokens retain their original spatial ordering; position IDs are re-indexed contiguously.

Model-specific retention ratios reflect each architecture's token count and expected sensitivity. $\pi_0$ is evaluated offline only ($k{=}0.1$, normalized L1 action loss; offline metrics only, no closed-loop evaluation available) because its deployment environment precludes closed-loop testing. $\pi_0.5$ uses $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7\}$ under both L2 and random removal; the dual-method design enables direct comparison within a single architecture. Pruning is applied per-image: each camera view independently retains the top-$k$ fraction. X-VLA spans $k \in \{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.8, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95\}$ with L2 removal across three seeds, supplemented by random removal at selected $k$ values. OpenVLA uses $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95\}$ with random removal as the primary protocol and L2 for direct comparison. SmolVLA tests seven configurations varying scoring (learned ACIS vs.\ random), retention ratio ($k \in \{0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95\}$), and mechanism (zero-masking, true removal, token merging via ToMe~\cite{bolya2023tome}).

The $\pi_0$--OpenVLA pair provides the closest available comparison: both consume 256 vision tokens from SigLIP-based encoders, but differ in connector (linear projection vs.\ three-layer MLP), backbone (PaliGemma vs.\ Llama-2), and encoder fusion strategy (single SigLIP vs.\ DINOv2+SigLIP). This comparison involves differences in connector, backbone, and encoder fusion, limiting causal interpretation; the primary evidence for the connector's role comes from the monotonic CV-degradation relationship across all seven models (Section~\ref{sec:scaling_law}). Retention ratio $k$ controls the fraction retained within each model, but absolute token counts vary across architectures at matched $k$: $k{=}0.7$ retains 35 tokens for X-VLA, 179 for OpenVLA, and 538 for $\pi_0.5$. The CV analysis derives from within-model comparisons where each model serves as its own control, eliminating this confounder.


