\begin{abstract}
Vision token pruning accelerates Vision Transformer inference with negligible accuracy loss, but whether this robustness transfers to Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models remains untested across architectures. We study seven VLA models (450M--7B parameters) spanning three vision connector families, evaluating four in closed-loop robotic manipulation (LIBERO) and analyzing three via offline metrics or literature. The vision connector is strongly associated with pruning behavior along a four-class compressibility spectrum. Uniformly critical models ($\pi_0$, $\pi_0.5$; CV $< 0.06$) tolerate random subsampling but collapse under importance-based selection: $\pi_0.5$ retains 69.4\% success rate with random removal at 50\% retention but drops to 33.4\% under L2 norm selection, a 36 percentage-point gap demonstrating that offline pruning tolerance does not guarantee closed-loop robustness. Threshold-graded models (X-VLA; CV $= 0.151$) maintain $97.8 \pm 0.4$\% through 90\% retention before cliffing at 85\% ($-$31\,pp). Smooth graded models (OpenVLA, OFT; CV $\approx 0.22$) degrade proportionally. Catastrophic cliff models (SmolVLA; CV $= 0.314$) collapse to 0\% at 5\% removal across all seven tested configurations. The coefficient of variation (CV) of token L2 norms serves as a zero-cost diagnostic metric that correlates with both the tolerance class and the optimal pruning strategy from a single forward pass. The gap between random and L2 norm selection scales monotonically with CV at both 50\% and 70\% retention across three models. For catastrophic-cliff architectures, Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR) recovers 60\%+ success rate at 75\% compression where all deletion methods yield 0\%, with a relatively stable M-curve ($M{=}16 \approx M{=}128$) confirming learned re-encoding as the operative mechanism. CV diagnosis followed by connector-appropriate compression forms a diagnostic protocol for safe VLA token reduction.
\end{abstract}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We studied vision token pruning across seven VLA models (450M--7B) spanning three connector families on closed-loop robotic manipulation. Four findings emerge. (1)~The vision connector is strongly associated with pruning behavior along a four-class compressibility spectrum: uniformly critical, threshold-graded, smooth graded, and catastrophic cliff. The monotonic CV-degradation relationship across all seven models is consistent with the connector being a primary factor, though confounded by backbone differences. (2)~Low-CV models are uniformly critical, not immune: $\pi_0.5$ shows a 36\,pp gap between random and L2 norm selection at $k{=}0.5$, demonstrating that offline pruning tolerance does not guarantee closed-loop robustness. (3)~Token importance CV serves as a zero-cost diagnostic metric that correlates with both tolerance class and optimal strategy from a single forward pass. (4)~Learned re-encoding (both cross-attention and MLP, $p = 0.15$) recovers 60\%+ SR at 75\% compression where all deletion methods yield 0\%.

These results yield a diagnostic protocol. First, compute CV from a single forward pass. Models below CV $\approx 0.15$ should use coverage-preserving methods; models above can use importance-based scoring; pixel-shuffle architectures require learned re-encoding. All closed-loop results use LIBERO simulation; $\pi_0$ evaluation is offline only; the CV correlation spans three models and two retention ratios. Code and evaluation scripts will be released upon publication.

