\subsection{CV Correlates with Optimal Pruning Strategy}
\label{sec:scaling_law}

The preceding sections establish that connector architecture is strongly associated with pruning tolerance. A natural follow-up: can a single diagnostic indicate not only \emph{how much} pruning a model tolerates, but also \emph{which} strategy to use? Token importance CV provides this as a diagnostic metric that correlates with both pruning tolerance and optimal strategy. Table~\ref{tab:scaling_law} compares L2 norm-based and random removal at two retention ratios ($k{=}0.5$ and $k{=}0.7$) across three models with measured CV.

\subsection{CV Correlates with Optimal Pruning Strategy}
\label{sec:scaling_law}

Token importance CV correlates with both pruning tolerance and optimal strategy. Comparing L2 norm-based and random removal across three models, the gap $\Delta$(R$-$L2) decreases monotonically with CV at both $k{=}0.5$ and $k{=}0.7$ (full data in Table~\ref{tab:scaling_law}, Appendix~\ref{app:cv_scaling_table}). At $k{=}0.5$: $+$36.0\,pp ($\pi_0.5$, CV$\,{=}\,$0.055) $\rightarrow$ $+$26.9\,pp (X-VLA, CV$\,{=}\,$0.151) $\rightarrow$ $-$1.8\,pp (OpenVLA, CV$\,{=}\,$0.215). At $k{=}0.7$: $+$22.2\,pp $\rightarrow$ $+$6.5\,pp $\rightarrow$ $-$5.4\,pp. The monotonic ordering holds across a 2$\times$ change in token budget, indicating CV captures a structural property of the connector.

When tokens have near-uniform norms (low CV), L2 ranking captures spatial artifacts rather than genuine importance, clustering retained tokens on correlated patches. Random selection preserves spatial uniformity. As CV rises, a genuine importance gradient emerges that L2 can exploit. Models below CV $\approx 0.15$ should use coverage-preserving methods; models above can benefit from importance-based scoring. At extreme compression ($k{=}0.3$), the interaction partially reverses (Appendix~\ref{app:cv_strategy_interaction}).

\section{CV Scaling Law: Full Data}
\label{app:cv_scaling_table}

